DNS Troubleshooting with nslookup

Date: October 7, 2026
Platform / Environment: Windows — IT Support / Networking

What I Learned

DNS translates names into IP addresses so devices can locate network resources.

I learned how nslookup can be used to directly test DNS resolution and help investigate problems that may initially appear to be general internet or network issues.

I also learned that:

* nslookup can test whether a DNS server can resolve a name.
* Internal Active Directory environments rely heavily on internal DNS.
* ipconfig /flushdns clears the local DNS cache; it does not repair a DNS server.
* Different tests provide different pieces of evidence, so troubleshooting shouldn’t rely on one command alone.

Key Takeaway

A complaint such as:

“I can’t access this website/server.”

isn’t specific enough to immediately identify the problem.

A better troubleshooting approach is to ask:

Does the machine have valid network configuration? Which DNS server is it using? Can that DNS server resolve the name?

From there, I can use the results to narrow down whether the problem is related to network configuration, DNS resolution, or something further along the connection.

Practical Relevance

DNS troubleshooting is particularly important for IT Support because DNS problems can easily look like broader connectivity problems.

This also connects directly to my previous networking learning around DHCP and ipconfig /all.
